\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Econometrics II - Exam 1 Solutions (2012)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Suppose the structural equation in 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},w_{i},q_{i})=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{i}+\beta_{2}w_{i}+\beta_{3}q_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

, but 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not observable.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i}q_{i})\neq0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose we don't observe any other variables.
 Is it possible to consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 by imposing any assumption?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain the condition(s) a valid proxy for 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 should satisfy.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is possible to consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

 by imposing the assumption 
\begin_inset Formula $L(q_{i}|x_{i},w_{i})=L(q_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 The key idea of the assumption is that, once we condition on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not partially correlated to 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 onto 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L(q_{i}|x_{i})=\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{i}+r_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(r_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Now, using the linear projection above, we can rewrite the structural equation
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{i} & = & \beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{i}+\beta_{2}w_{i}+\beta_{3}(\delta_{0}+\delta_{1}x_{i}+r_{i})+u_{i}\\
y_{i} & = & \left(\beta_{0}+\beta_{3}\delta_{0}\right)+\left(\beta_{1}+\delta_{1}\right)x_{i}+\beta_{2}w_{i}+\left(u_{i}+\beta_{3}r_{i}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i}r_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, then we can consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A valid proxy 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 should satisfy the following conditions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}|x_{i},w_{i},q_{i},z_{i})=E(y_{i}|x_{i},w_{i},q_{i})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $L(q_{i}|x_{i},w_{i},z_{i})=L(q_{i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{i}=x_{i}\delta+w_{i}\lambda+\epsilon_{i},\ (1=1,\ldots,n)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 are scalars, 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}\epsilon_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i}\epsilon_{i})\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 Additionally we observe an IV 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}w_{i})\neq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}\epsilon_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Consider the estimator 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{\delta},\hat{\lambda})$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\delta,\lambda)$
\end_inset

 which is obtained as 2SLS except that the first stage regresses 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 solely on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 (instead of both 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

).
 Is the estimator consistent? If not, under which conditions is it consistent?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If we multiply equation (1) by the two exogenous variables and take expectation,
 then by the moment conditions, we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(x_{i}y_{i}) & = & E(x_{i}^{2})\delta+E(x_{i}w_{i})\lambda\\
E(z_{i}y_{i}) & = & E(z_{i}x_{i})\delta+E(z_{i}w_{i})\lambda
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We write these two equations in matrix form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
E(x_{i}^{2}) & E(x_{i}w_{i})\\
E(z_{i}x_{i}) & E(z_{i}w_{i})
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\delta\\
\lambda
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
E(x_{i}y_{i})\\
E(z_{i}y_{i})
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

So, we can obtain an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $(\delta,\lambda)$
\end_inset

 in terms of the population moment conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
\delta\\
\lambda
\end{array}\right] & = & \frac{1}{E(x_{i}^{2})E(z_{i}w_{i})-E(x_{i}w_{i})E(z_{i}x_{i})}\left[\begin{array}{cc}
E(z_{i}w_{i}) & -E(x_{i}w_{i})\\
-E(z_{i}x_{i}) & E(x_{i}^{2})
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
E(x_{i}y_{i})\\
E(z_{i}y_{i})
\end{array}\right]\\
\delta & = & \frac{E(z_{i}w_{i})E(x_{i}y_{i})-E(x_{i}w_{i})E(z_{i}y_{i})}{E(x_{i}^{2})E(z_{i}w_{i})-E(x_{i}w_{i})E(z_{i}x_{i})}\\
\lambda & = & \frac{E(x_{i}^{2})E(z_{i}y_{i})-E(z_{i}x_{i})E(x_{i}y_{i})}{E(x_{i}^{2})E(z_{i}w_{i})-E(x_{i}w_{i})E(z_{i}x_{i})}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, suppose we obtain a 2SLS estimator where we regress 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 solely on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the first stage to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{w}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}z_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Then, replacing 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i}$
\end_inset

, the model is rewritten as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=x_{i}\delta+\hat{w}_{i}\lambda+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, then multiplying this equation by 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{w}_{i}$
\end_inset

 and taking expectation yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(x_{i}y_{i}) & = & E(x_{i}^{2})\delta+E(x_{i}\hat{w}_{i})\lambda\\
E(\hat{w}_{i}y_{i}) & = & E(\hat{w}_{i}x_{i})\delta+E(\hat{w}_{i}^{2})\lambda
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We write these two equations in matrix form
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
E(x_{i}^{2}) & E(x_{i}\hat{w}_{i})\\
E(\hat{w}_{i}x_{i}) & E(\hat{w}_{i}^{2})
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\delta\\
\lambda
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
E(x_{i}y_{i})\\
E(\hat{w}_{i}y_{i})
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

and take the sample analogue
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[\begin{array}{cc}
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2} & n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\hat{w}_{i}\\
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}x_{i} & n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}^{2}
\end{array}\right]\left[\begin{array}{c}
\hat{\delta}\\
\hat{\lambda}
\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}y_{i}\\
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}y_{i}
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset

Solving for 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{\delta},\hat{\lambda})$
\end_inset

 yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left[\begin{array}{c}
\hat{\delta}\\
\hat{\lambda}
\end{array}\right] & = & \frac{1}{\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}^{2}\right)-\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}x_{i}\right)^{2}}\left[\begin{array}{cc}
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}^{2} & -n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}x_{i}\\
-n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}x_{i} & n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}
\end{array}\right]\\
 &  & \times\left[\begin{array}{c}
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}y_{i}\\
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}y_{i}
\end{array}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, we now have an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\delta}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\delta} & = & \frac{\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}y_{i}\right)-\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\hat{w}_{i}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}y_{i}\right)}{\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}^{2}\right)-\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\hat{w}_{i}\right)^{2}}\\
 & = & \frac{\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)^{2}\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}y_{i}\right)-\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)^{2}\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}z_{i}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}y_{i}\right)}{\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)^{2}\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}\right)-\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)^{2}\left[n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}z_{i}\right]^{2}}\\
 & = & \frac{\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}y_{i}\right)-\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}z_{i}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}y_{i}\right)}{\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}\right)-\left[n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}z_{i}\right]^{2}}\\
 & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \frac{E(z_{i}^{2})E(x_{i}y_{i})-E(x_{i}z_{i})E(z_{i}y_{i})}{E(x_{i}^{2})E(z_{i}^{2})-\left[E(x_{i}z_{i})\right]^{2}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\hat{\lambda} & = & \frac{\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}y_{i}\right)-\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}x_{i}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}y_{i}\right)}{\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{w}_{i}^{2}\right)-\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\hat{w}_{i}\right)^{2}}\\
 & = & \frac{\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}y_{i}\right)-\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}x_{i}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}y_{i}\right)}{\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)^{2}\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}\right)-\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)^{2}\left[n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}z_{i}\right]^{2}}\\
 & = & \frac{\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}y_{i}\right)-\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}x_{i}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}y_{i}\right)}{\left(\frac{n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}\right)\left(n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}\right)-\left(\frac{n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}w_{i}}{n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}^{2}}\right)\left[n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}z_{i}\right]^{2}}\\
 & \begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array} & \frac{E(x_{i}^{2})E(z_{i}y_{i})-E(z_{i}x_{i})E(x_{i}y_{i})}{\left(\frac{E(z_{i}w_{i})}{E(z_{i}^{2})}\right)E(z_{i}^{2})E(x_{i}^{2})-\left(\frac{E(z_{i}w_{i})}{E(z_{i}^{2})}\right)\left[E(z_{i}x_{i})\right]^{2}}\\
 & = & \frac{E(x_{i}^{2})E(z_{i}y_{i})-E(z_{i}x_{i})E(x_{i}y_{i})}{E(z_{i}w_{i})E(x_{i}^{2})-\left(\frac{E(z_{i}w_{i})}{E(z_{i}^{2})}\right)\left[E(z_{i}x_{i})\right]^{2}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, in general, the estimator is inconsistent.
 However, under the condition that 
\begin_inset Formula $E(w_{i}x_{i})=E(x_{i}z_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, then the estimator will be consistent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider the 2SLS regression (all variables are univariate): 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=x_{i}\beta+\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=z_{i}\delta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is endogenous and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a valid IV for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the OLS fitted value of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 in the first stage.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be the OLS estimator in the second stage and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mbox{se}}_{\beta}$
\end_inset

 be the correponding OLS standard error [i.e., having the form of 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{x}_{i}^{2}\right)^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2}=n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\check{\epsilon}_{i}^{2}$
\end_inset

 with the OLS residual 
\begin_inset Formula $\check{\epsilon}_{i}=y_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

] We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is called the 2SLS estimator and consistently estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 in the presence of endogeneity.
 Show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\mbox{se}}_{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is not a valid standard error for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and provide a correct one.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, consider the OLS residual specfied in the problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\check{\epsilon}_{i} & = & y_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}\\
 & = & x_{i}\beta+\epsilon_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}\\
 & = & x_{i}\beta+\epsilon_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}\hat{\beta}+x_{i}\hat{\beta}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}\\
 & = & \epsilon_{i}+x_{i}(\beta-\hat{\beta})+\hat{\beta}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})\\
\check{\epsilon}_{i}^{2} & = & \epsilon_{i}^{2}+x_{i}^{2}(\beta-\hat{\beta})^{2}+\hat{\beta}^{2}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})^{2}+2\epsilon_{i}x_{i}(\beta-\hat{\beta})+2\epsilon_{i}\hat{\beta}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})+2x_{i}\hat{\beta}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})(\beta-\hat{\beta})\\
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\check{\epsilon}_{i}^{2} & = & n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\epsilon_{i}^{2}+n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{2}(\beta-\hat{\beta})^{2}+n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{\beta}^{2}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})^{2}\\
 &  & +n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}2\epsilon_{i}x_{i}(\beta-\hat{\beta})+n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}2\epsilon_{i}\hat{\beta}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})+n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}2x_{i}\hat{\beta}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})(\beta-\hat{\beta})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, by the law of large numbers
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\check{\epsilon}_{i}^{2}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}+E\left[\hat{\beta}^{2}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})^{2}\right]+2E\left[\epsilon_{i}\hat{\beta}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})\right]
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $E\left[\hat{\beta}^{2}(x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i})^{2}\right]\neq0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\check{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2}=n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\check{\epsilon}_{i}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

is not a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We know that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2}=n^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\hat{\epsilon}_{i}^{2}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\epsilon}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is a consistent estimator for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}$
\end_inset

, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\sigma}_{\epsilon}^{2}\begin{array}[b]{c}
_{p}\\
\rightarrow
\end{array}\sigma_{\epsilon}^{2}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a system with 
\begin_inset Formula $G$
\end_inset

 equations 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{gi}=x_{gi}^{\prime}\beta_{g}+\epsilon_{gi},\ g=1,\ldots,G$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{g}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{g}\times1$
\end_inset

.
 The first element of each 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{gi}$
\end_inset

 is one so the first element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{g}$
\end_inset

 is in the intercept.
 The set of instruments available to the 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

-th equation is 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{gi}$
\end_inset

, whihc is 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{g}\times1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Describe the multiple-equation two-step optimal GMM estimator for 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{2}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Provide a detailed algorithm to test the hypothesis of common intercept
 (over equations).
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
First, we obtain a consistent estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{GMM}=\left(S_{xz}S_{zz}^{-1}S_{zx}\right)^{-1}\left(S_{xz}S_{zz}^{-1}S_{zx}\right)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{zx}=n^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{n}z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{zy}=n^{-1}\sum z_{i}y_{i}$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
z_{i} & = & \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
z_{1i} &  & 0\\
 & \ddots\\
0 &  & z_{Gi}
\end{array}\right]_{\sum L_{g}\times G}\\
x_{i} & = & \left[\begin{array}{ccc}
x_{1i} &  & 0\\
 & \ddots\\
0 &  & x_{Gi}
\end{array}\right]_{\sum K_{g}\times G}\\
y_{i} & = & \left[\begin{array}{c}
y_{1i}\\
\vdots\\
y_{Gi}
\end{array}\right]_{G\times1}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

This is to calculate the GMM residuals 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\epsilon}_{i}=y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\beta}_{GMM}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Using the residuals obtained in the first step, we calculate the optimal
 weighting matrix
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{S}=\left(n^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}z_{i}\hat{\epsilon}_{i}\hat{\epsilon}_{i}{}_{i}^{\prime}z_{i}^{\prime}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Finally, we use this weighting matrix in the GMM estimator to obtain the
 optimal GMM estimator
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{GMM}^{opt}=\left(S_{xz}\hat{S}^{-1}S_{zx}\right)\left(S_{xz}\hat{S}^{-1}S_{zy}\right)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\beta_{g}=\left[\begin{array}{c}
\beta_{1g}\\
\vdots\\
\beta_{Kg}
\end{array}\right]_{K_{g}\times1}
\]

\end_inset

We wish to test the null hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{0}:\beta_{11}=\beta_{12}=\cdots=\beta_{1G}
\]

\end_inset

against
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
H_{1}:H_{0}\ \mbox{is false}
\]

\end_inset

Calculate the Wald Statistic
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q=(R\hat{\beta}-q)^{\prime}\left(R\hat{V}R^{\prime}\right)^{-1}\left(R\hat{\beta}-q\right)\sim\chi^{2}(G-1)\ \mbox{under}\ H_{0}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
R & = & \left[\begin{array}{ccccccccc}
1 & \cdots & 0 & -1 & 0 &  & \cdots\\
0 & \cdots & 0 & 1 & 0 & \cdots & 0 & -1 & \cdots\\
0 &  &  & \cdots &  &  & 0 & 1 & \cdots\\
 &  &  &  & \vdots
\end{array}\right]_{\left(K_{G-1}+1\right)\times G}\\
\hat{V} & = & \left(S_{xz}\hat{S}^{-1}S_{zx}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We reject 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 is greater than the critical value 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 of significance 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $P(Q>Q_{\alpha}|H_{0})=\alpha$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Describe one example each for identified and non-identified models (with
 assumptions) in econometrics.
 Explain why.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the single-equation linear model with exogenous regressors
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $K\times1$
\end_inset

.
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 to be identified, the following 2 assumptions need to be satisfied:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assumption 1: 
\begin_inset Formula $E(x_{i}u_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption allows us to consistently estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})\beta=E(x_{i}y_{i})
\]

\end_inset

Assumption 2: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ E(x_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=K$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption allows us to identify the unique 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 to estimate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With these two assumptions, the model is identified.
 However, if either of the two assumptions fail, then the model is longer
 identified.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider the single equation model 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+\epsilon_{i},\ i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

.
 Part of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 could be endogenous and we have IVs 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{dim}(z_{i})=L\geq K=\mbox{dim}(x_{i})=\mbox{dim}(\beta)$
\end_inset

.
 Denote the Wald test statistic (based on the 2SLS estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, called 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}$
\end_inset

) for 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\beta=1_{K\times1}$
\end_inset

 as 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

.
 Describe the asymptotic behavior of 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 under the alternative:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}:\beta=2_{K\times1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}:\beta=\nicefrac{2_{K\times1}}{\sqrt{n}}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $H_{1}:\beta=\nicefrac{2_{K\times1}}{n}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The Wald statistic under 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Q=\sqrt{n}\left(R\hat{\beta}-1_{K\times1}\right)^{\prime}\left[R\hat{V}R^{\prime}\right]^{-1}\sqrt{n}\left(R\hat{\beta}-1_{K\times1}\right)\sim\chi^{2}(K)
\]

\end_inset

Note that in this setup, 
\begin_inset Formula $R=I_{K}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{V}=\left(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}\right)^{-1}\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\epsilon_{i}^{2}\hat{x}_{i}\hat{x}_{i}^{\prime}\right)\left(\hat{X}^{\prime}\hat{X}\right)^{-1}
\]

\end_inset

To describe the asymptotic behavior of the statistic 
\begin_inset Formula $Q$
\end_inset

 under the alternatives, we rewrite the statistic as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
Q & = & \sqrt{n}\left(R\hat{\beta}-q_{0}+q_{1}-q_{1}\right)^{\prime}\left[R\hat{V}R^{\prime}\right]^{-1}\sqrt{n}\left(R\hat{\beta}-q_{0}+q_{1}-q_{1}\right)\\
Q & = & \left[\sqrt{n}\left(R\hat{\beta}-q_{1}\right)+\sqrt{n}(q_{1}-q_{0})\right]^{\prime}\left[R\hat{V}R^{\prime}\right]^{-1}\left[\sqrt{n}\left(R\hat{\beta}-q_{1}\right)+\sqrt{n}(q_{1}-q_{0})\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

